jtest: the ext2fs journal on a Linux host
=========================================

Builds ext2fs/journal.c straight from the Hurd tree (HURD=, default
../cross-hurd/src/hurd), unchanged, and runs it against a fake ext2fs:

  shim/        A fake Mach and the <hurd/...> types the real Hurd lib
               headers need.  libdiskfs, libports, libstore, libihash and
               ext2fs headers are the real ones, so struct node, struct
               store, diskfs_transaction_t are what ext2fs sees.
  env/         The fake ext2fs (fakefs.c) and the harness API (jtest.h):
               - the store is the image in memory, and every write and
                 sync also goes to a write log;
               - the disk cache maps every block; blocks are resident or
                 evicted, and tests edit them with jt_edit, which faults
                 an evicted block back in;
               - pager threads serve clean/evict page-outs through
                 journal_store_write and page-ins through
                 journal_store_read; a page-in must return exactly the
                 bytes that were evicted, or it counts as a stale read.
  env/crash.c  jt_crash_check: rebuilds the image after prefixes of the
               write log (and with the last write torn), replays each with
               the real e2fsck -fy, requires e2fsck -fn clean, then runs
               the test's own check.  Images are checked in parallel in
               /dev/shm.  A failure keeps the crashed image, e2fsck's
               replay of it, and a decoded write log (JBD2 headers,
               descriptor tags, home writes) under build/.
  tests/       t_basic: many transactions under page-outs and evictions,
               clean unmount, every block home, e2fsck -fn clean.
               t_crash: one handle per transaction, each committed.
               t_wrap: goes round the smallest log ~8 times; forced
               checkpoints, and handles larger than a transaction may be.
               t_threads: 4 RPC threads share transactions, with nested
               starts and a shared block edited under a lock.
               Each crash image must hold whole operations only, and
               every operation stopped before a commit that returned.
  patches/     Fixes found with the harness, against ext2fs/journal.c.

  make check                      build everything and run the tests
  make check SAN=address,undefined
  make check SAN=thread
  make check JOURNAL_C=patched/journal.c   test a modified copy
  JT_CRASH_POINTS=0 build/t_wrap  # every crash point (default: 300)
  JT_NO_CRASH=1 build/t_threads 7 # run seed 7, skip the crash images
  JT_JOBS=1, JT_TMP=dir           # crash-check workers, image directory

To try a patch:
  mkdir -p patched && cp $HURD/ext2fs/journal.c patched/ &&
  patch -d patched -p2 < patches/empty-log-superblock.diff

A test's stderr (journal warnings, statistics, the flight recorder dump)
goes to build/t_NAME.log.  Needs gcc and e2fsprogs on the host.

The pager follows libpager: writes of a block are serialized, and a
page-in that comes while the block is being written out gets the data
being written (PM_PAGEINWAIT) instead of reading it.

What is not modelled: the file pagers (journal_*_external, directory
blocks), the allocator and busy blocks beyond a callback, pages larger
than a block, and anything above the journal API.  Test images are
mke2fs -t ext3 (indirect-mapped journal inode, no extents).
